{"skill":{"slug":"peekaboo","displayName":"Peekaboo"},"version":{"version":"1.0.0","createdAt":1767545373660,"changelog":"","changelogSource":null,"license":null,"files":[{"path":"SKILL.md","size":5753,"sha256":"7a87a01399f99780e872b16f3df999d0e684a3b8dbc189cd20b9cc4230762ed5","contentType":"text/markdown"},{"path":"skill-card.md","size":1932,"sha256":"3a9c5c8198a333e9dfc52fc8f3c39eeadfc363dc7ab15f030685785d11354968","contentType":"text/markdown"}],"security":{"status":"suspicious","hasWarnings":true,"checkedAt":1789013609991,"model":null,"hasScanResult":true,"sha256hash":"0bc69020cf7935033dfde24a43a5b8c10aa59e9ed7fbf729a5448fa9d3a5639d","virustotalUrl":"https://www.virustotal.com/gui/file/0bc69020cf7935033dfde24a43a5b8c10aa59e9ed7fbf729a5448fa9d3a5639d","scanners":{"vt":{"status":"suspicious","verdict":"suspicious","normalizedStatus":"suspicious","analysis":"Type: OpenClaw Skill\nName: peekaboo\nVersion: 1.0.0\n\nThe skill bundle itself is descriptive and does not contain direct malicious instructions or prompt injection attempts. However, it exposes the `peekaboo` CLI tool, which grants extensive and high-risk capabilities on a macOS system. These include reading/writing the clipboard (text, images, files), capturing screenshots, launching arbitrary applications/files/URLs via the `open` command, simulating all user input, and potentially handling credentials via its `config` command. While these capabilities are plausible for a UI automation tool, their broad nature and potential for misuse classify the bundle as suspicious due to the inherent risks if the agent were to be compromised or misused.","source":"palm","checkedAt":1779159622667},"skillspector":{"status":"clean","normalizedStatus":"clean","score":8,"severity":"LOW","recommendation":"SAFE","issueCount":1,"checkedAt":1789013609991},"llm":{"status":"suspicious","verdict":"suspicious","normalizedStatus":"suspicious","confidence":"medium","summary":"This skill is a coherent macOS automation helper, but it grants broad screen, clipboard, dialog, and input-control abilities with limited safety guidance.","dimensions":[{"detail":"Screen Recording and Accessibility permissions are expected for this tool, but they are high-impact permissions that can expose visible private data and automate actions across applications.","label":"Credentials","name":"environment_proportionality","rating":"concern"},{"detail":"The skill installs the required CLI from the third-party Homebrew tap steipete/tap/peekaboo without pinning a reviewed version or checksum; this is a supply-chain consideration rather than evidence of malicious behavior.","label":"Install Mechanism","name":"install_mechanism","rating":"note"},{"detail":"The instructions disclose broad automation powers but provide only minimal safety guidance, despite examples that can interact with login windows and type sensitive values.","label":"Instruction Scope","name":"instruction_scope","rating":"concern"},{"detail":"The artifact contains only a Markdown skill file and install metadata; it does not define background services, startup persistence, privilege escalation, or hidden executable payloads.","label":"Persistence & Privilege","name":"persistence_privilege","rating":"ok"},{"detail":"The documented capabilities fit the stated purpose of using Peekaboo for macOS UI capture and automation, including screenshots, app/window control, clipboard access, dialogs, and typed/clicked input.","label":"Purpose & Capability","name":"purpose_capability","rating":"note"}],"guidance":"Install only if you trust the Peekaboo package source and are comfortable granting macOS Screen Recording and Accessibility permissions. Avoid using command-line examples with real passwords or other secrets, and review each automation action before letting an agent click, type, read the clipboard, or interact with dialogs on your behalf.","findings":"[SQP-2] expected: The broad macOS UI automation abilities are purpose-aligned and disclosed, but the artifact gives little user-facing warning about privacy exposure, destructive UI actions, or sensitive dialogs.\n[T08] expected: Using Homebrew to install the Peekaboo CLI is consistent with the skill's purpose, but the unpinned third-party tap means later installs may fetch code different from what was reviewed.\n[T09] unexpected: The login example types a placeholder password directly through a shell command argument; this is not needed to demonstrate UI automation and could encourage unsafe handling of real credentials.","agenticRiskFindings":null,"riskSummary":null,"model":null,"checkedAt":1789013609991}}}}}